Yes. This is worth documenting because **the CA trust has to exist in different trust stores depending on who is connecting to `harbor.local`**.

In our setup, we had several different clients:

```text
                         harbor.local
                              │
              ┌───────────────┼────────────────┐
              │               │                │
           Host OS          Docker          Minikube/K8s
              │               │                │
        curl / browser    docker push      signer/verifier
              │               │                │
       OS CA store      Docker CA store    K8s CA/ConfigMap
```

So copying the CA to one place **does not automatically make every component trust it**.

## Harbor Local CA Trust — Reusable Setup Notes

### 1. CA files

Our local Harbor CA is:

```bash
~/harbor-pki/ca.crt
```

Check it:

```bash
ls -l ~/harbor-pki/ca.crt
```

If you create another Harbor installation, first identify the CA file generated for that Harbor.

---

### 2. Host OS trust store

This is needed for programs on Ubuntu that use the system CA bundle, such as `curl`.

Install the CA:

```bash
sudo cp ~/harbor-pki/ca.crt /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/harbor-ca.crt
sudo update-ca-certificates
```

Test:

```bash
curl -v https://harbor.local/ >/dev/null
```

The important part is:

```text
SSL certificate verify ok.
```

and:

```text
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
```

You can also test without all the verbose output:

```bash
curl -sS https://harbor.local/ >/dev/null && echo "Harbor TLS trusted"
```

---

### 3. Docker trust store

This is **separate from the Ubuntu CA store**.

For Docker to trust a private CA when doing:

```bash
docker push harbor.local/...
```

put the CA here:

```bash
sudo mkdir -p /etc/docker/certs.d/harbor.local
sudo cp ~/harbor-pki/ca.crt \
  /etc/docker/certs.d/harbor.local/ca.crt
```

Verify that it is the same CA:

```bash
sudo sha256sum /etc/docker/certs.d/harbor.local/ca.crt
sha256sum ~/harbor-pki/ca.crt
```

The hashes should be identical.

Then restart Docker:

```bash
sudo systemctl restart docker
```

Check:

```bash
sudo systemctl is-active docker
```

Expected:

```text
active
```

And:

```bash
docker info >/dev/null && echo "Docker is responding"
```

Then test the actual registry operation:

```bash
docker push harbor.local/demo/signer-test:signer-k8s-test
```

A successful push should end with something like:

```text
digest: sha256:...
```

### Important

When we restarted Docker, **all Docker containers temporarily stopped**, including Harbor and the Minikube Docker container.

So after restarting Docker, check:

```bash
docker compose ps
minikube status
```

If Minikube is stopped:

```bash
minikube start
```

Also remember that Minikube may require enough disk space before it can start.

---

### 4. Kubernetes / Minikube trust

This is different again.

Your Kubernetes applications such as:

```text
image-signer
image-verifier
```

run **inside containers**.

The Ubuntu CA store:

```text
/usr/local/share/ca-certificates/
```

doesn't automatically exist inside those containers.

For our application, we created a Kubernetes ConfigMap containing the Harbor CA:

```bash
kubectl get configmap harbor-ca -n artifact-platform
```

The application pod mounts it at:

```text
/etc/harbor-ca
```

You can verify:

```bash
kubectl exec -n artifact-platform deploy/image-signer -- \
  ls -l /etc/harbor-ca
```

You should see the CA file.

---

### 5. The signer/verifier also need Cosign to trust/use the CA

This is particularly important.

There are actually **two separate concepts**:

```text
TLS trust
   ↓
Can the application securely connect to https://harbor.local ?

Cosign signing
   ↓
Can Cosign push/read the signature artifact from Harbor?
```

The signer has the Harbor CA mounted because Cosign needs to communicate with Harbor over HTTPS.

So check the deployment:

```bash
kubectl describe pod -n artifact-platform -l app=image-signer
```

Look for:

```text
/etc/harbor-ca
```

and:

```text
harbor-ca
```

---

## 6. Verify Harbor certificate itself

From the host:

```bash
openssl s_client \
  -connect harbor.local:443 \
  -servername harbor.local \
  -CAfile ~/harbor-pki/ca.crt \
  </dev/null
```

At the end, you want:

```text
Verify return code: 0 (ok)
```

This is a very useful test because it directly checks:

```text
harbor.local certificate
        ↓
signed by our CA
        ↓
CA file is trusted
```

---

## 7. Verify the hostname

The certificate must contain:

```text
harbor.local
```

as a SAN.

Check:

```bash
openssl s_client \
  -connect harbor.local:443 \
  -servername harbor.local \
  -CAfile ~/harbor-pki/ca.crt \
  </dev/null 2>/dev/null \
  | openssl x509 -noout -subject -issuer -ext subjectAltName
```

You should see something containing:

```text
DNS:harbor.local
```

This matters because **trusting the CA is not enough**. The certificate also has to match the hostname you're connecting to.

---

# 8. Quick checklist for creating another local Harbor

When you create another local Harbor with a private CA, think:

```text
[1] Generate/use Harbor CA
        │
        ├── ~/harbor-pki/ca.crt
        │
        ├── [2] Ubuntu host trust
        │       /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/
        │
        ├── [3] Docker trust
        │       /etc/docker/certs.d/<harbor-hostname>/ca.crt
        │
        ├── [4] Kubernetes trust
        │       ConfigMap harbor-ca
        │
        └── [5] Application/Cosign
                mount CA into signer/verifier
```

And verify each layer separately:

```bash
# Host
curl -v https://harbor.local/

# Certificate
openssl s_client \
  -connect harbor.local:443 \
  -servername harbor.local \
  -CAfile ~/harbor-pki/ca.crt \
  </dev/null

# Docker
docker push harbor.local/demo/test:tag

# Kubernetes CA
kubectl get configmap harbor-ca -n artifact-platform

# Application mount
kubectl exec -n artifact-platform deploy/image-signer -- \
  ls -l /etc/harbor-ca
```

### The key thing to remember

**Don't think of "the machine trusts my CA" as one global setting.**

Think:

> **Every TLS client needs access to the CA in the trust store/container where that client runs.**

In our project:

```text
Ubuntu curl
    → Ubuntu CA store

Docker CLI / Docker Engine
    → /etc/docker/certs.d/harbor.local/ca.crt

Kubernetes workloads
    → Kubernetes Secret/ConfigMap + mounted CA

Cosign inside image-signer
    → CA available inside signer container
```

That's why we had to put the CA in several places.
